  "question": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/620cbb55-b13d-4232-bd26-7227f06e3b6f-image.png\" style=\"width: 198px; height: 213px;\" /></p><p>A uniform rod of length &#39; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>&#8467;</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;is pivoted at one of its ends on a vertical shaft of negligible radius. When the shaft rotates at angular speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#969;</mi></math> the rod makes an angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi></math>with it (see figure). To find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi></math> equate the rate of change of angular momentum (direction going into the paper) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>&#8467;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>12</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></math> about the centre of mass (CM) to the torque provided by the horizontal and vertical forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></math>and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></math> about the CM. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi></math> is then such that:</p>",
    "image": null
